homelab-search (0.2.28) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Honour drive's per-file exclude_from_search as well as the folder
    flag. Effective non-searchability is the OR of a file's own flag and
    every folder above it, so clearing a file's flag does not un-hide it
    inside a private folder.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 01:26:11 -0600

homelab-search (0.2.27) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Honour drive's exclude_from_search flag (migration 010). Files in a
    non-searchable folder are STILL INDEXED - their text and metadata
    are held, and they still open in drive - but they never come back
    from a search. The filter is unconditional: there is no caller flag
    that turns it off, because an 'include private results' option is
    how it ends up on a shared screen.
  * The flag is carried onto the indexed row rather than joined from
    drive at query time: this schema holds no grant on drive.*, by
    design. Defaults to searchable, because defaulting the other way
    would empty everyone's results until a re-index finished. Exclusion
    applies to the whole folder subtree, and discovery re-queues any
    file whose searchability disagrees with drive, so ticking the box
    takes effect on the next pass.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 01:07:04 -0600

homelab-search (0.2.26) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix ancestor tag search. A photo is linked only to the most specific
    tag it carries - the ancestors exist as rows but nothing points at
    them - so matching on the tag NODE found 'Utah' and then no photos,
    while 'Moab' and 'Arches National Park' worked. Matching instead on
    whether the photo's tag PATH contains the term as a segment is the
    question that has an answer; every level of the tree now resolves.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 01:24:31 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.25) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Normalize photo people and the tag tree (migration 009). 007 stored
    tagged people as one text value - 'Ada Lovelace, Grace Hopper' in a
    single column - which is a repeating group in a field, and
    inconsistent with this schema's own treatment of mail participants.
    Four tables now: people, photo_faces (one row per tagged region,
    with its bounding box), tags (the hierarchy, with parent_id and a
    materialized path), and photo_tags.
  * Reading HierarchicalSubject, which is the tag tree a person built by
    hand in their photo manager - Places|Earth|USA|Utah|Moab|Arches
    National Park. The flat Keywords field holds only the leaf, so Moab
    was findable and Utah was not, even though they filed it under Utah
    themselves.
  * Face regions come back as INDEX-ALIGNED PARALLEL ARRAYS: face N is
    RegionName[N] with the box at RegionArea{X,Y,W,H}[N]. Reading the
    name list alone throws away which box belongs to whom. Areas are
    stored as fractions of the image, as the spec defines them, so a
    resized copy does not move every box.
  * Person and tag filters on drive search: an indexed join rather than
    a LIKE over a joined string, which also matched Jefferson when you
    searched Jeff. A tag matches the node and everything under it.
  * Names and tag paths feed the search vector, so Utah, Moab and Arches
    each match the photo filed under all three.
  * Migration gotcha worth recording: FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY subjects
    the table OWNER to its policies, so a migration touching account-
    scoped data matches zero rows unless it sets the indexer GUC first.
    The dry run caught it as a foreign-key failure on the dependent
    delete.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 01:20:48 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.24) unstable; urgency=medium

  * GPS is no longer withheld from AI mode (migration 008). A product
    decision: coordinates without a face carry little on their own, and
    every request from this service shares one Anthropic workspace, so a
    request is not labelled with whose it is. Recorded in the migration
    with the counter-argument that was weighed and overruled - phone GPS
    plus timestamps is a home address without a face, and within one
    request the points are linkable to each other.
  * people.names stays sensitive. It was not part of that decision: a
    name identifies a third party directly, and the people tagged in
    somebody's photos never saw the consent notice because they are not
    users.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:53:21 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.23) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Make file metadata SEARCHABLE, not just stored. Every text metadata
    value - tagged people, keywords, caption, camera, document title and
    author, a zip's member list - now feeds the drive search vector via
    tsv_source, while body_text stays the document so snippets and
    excerpts are unaffected.
  * This was a real gap: an image has no body text at all, so a tagged
    person's name was stored and shown on the page but could not be
    found by typing it. 'The zip with the invoice in it' is the plan's
    own example of what this should find, and it did not.
  * Kind-driven rather than a list of keys, so a text key added later is
    searchable the day it is added. Numbers and dates are left out: 1920
    and 2026-08-09 are filters, not search terms.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:28:35 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.22) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Stop indexing DJI's capture mode as a keyword. 'single' (one frame
    per shutter press) and 'smart' (SmartPhoto, where the aircraft picks
    HDR or Hyperlight) are real camera settings written into XPKeywords,
    a field that exists for keywords a person chose - so most of a drone
    library carried a meaningless 'Keywords: single'. Scoped to DJI by
    camera make, because those are ordinary words and somebody else's
    tag is their own.
  * Silence Archive::Zip's stderr handler: a corrupt archive is a normal
    thing to be sent, and it was printing a line per hostile upload as
    well as returning the status the code acts on.
  * Tests for the face-tag standards and the keyword rule, using a
    minimal JPEG inlined as base64 so the repository carries no
    binaries.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:26:17 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.21) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Drop camera factory placeholders from image descriptions. Every DJI
    photo in this corpus carries ImageDescription 'default', which put
    an identical meaningless line under 82 photos; Olympus and several
    phones stamp their own. Matched on the whole value and only for
    descriptions, so a photo actually captioned 'default' keeps its
    caption and a keyword is left alone.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:15:19 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.20) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Index the people tagged in a photo, plus keywords and descriptions.
    All three face-tagging standards are read - MWG Regions (Lightroom,
    digiKam, Picasa), Microsoft People Tags, and IPTC Extension
    PersonInImage - so whichever application wrote the tags, the names
    land in one key. Round-tripped against all three.
  * people.names is SENSITIVE, like GPS: a name attached to a photo is
    PII about somebody who is not the account holder and never agreed to
    anything. The owner searches it; it is never sent to a third party.
    people.count is not sensitive, so 'photos with people in them' stays
    answerable without naming anyone.
  * Fix: _text refused arrayrefs, which is exactly what a multi-face
    photo returns - three tagged faces is three RegionNames. It would
    have silently dropped the tagged photos and kept the untagged ones.
  * EXTRACTOR_VERSION 1 -> 2, which re-queues every drive file through
    the existing work queue. This is the first real exercise of that
    mechanism.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:13:10 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.19) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Per-type file metadata (plan phase 5): EXIF from images,
    duration/codec/dimensions from video and audio, title/author/page
    count from PDF and Office, and the member list from archives - so
    'the zip with the invoice in it' is findable.
  * Normalized into search.metadata_keys + search.drive_metadata rather
    than a JSONB blob: a key is a row, the value is indexed and typed,
    and the key - not the row - decides which of the three value columns
    is used.
  * content_created_at now comes from EXIF DateTimeOriginal where there
    is one. Before this, 8 of 30,499 drive items had any real creation
    date; a photo's upload time is not when it was taken.
  * GPS is stored and shown to the owner but NEVER sent to a third
    party: metadata_keys.sensitive is filtered in SQL by
    Query::metadata_for('safe'), which is what AI mode's drive tool
    calls. The live corpus already contains a drone photo with the exact
    spot it was taken.
  * Extraction is adversarial-input work: a wall-clock alarm, no shell,
    every value capped, and any failure means the file is indexed on its
    basic fields rather than failing the unit.
  * drive_items.extractor_version makes a re-index incremental - bump
    Extract::EXTRACTOR_VERSION and discovery re-queues stale files
    through the existing work queue, capped per pass so a bump cannot
    starve mail indexing.
  * Three bugs found by running it: a 256 MB size cap silently skipped
    every video (ExifTool reads headers, so a 564 MB file costs under a
    millisecond); passing an explicit tag list broke COMPOSITE tags, so
    videos indexed with dimensions but no duration; and the numeric
    parser read '1e400' as the number 1 instead of rejecting it.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 00:04:48 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.18) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a 500 when AI mode returned results: _resolve declared 'my
    (@out, $dropped) = ((), 0)', and in a list assignment the array is
    greedy - @out swallowed the 0, so every answer began with the
    integer 0 where the template expected a row, and the drop counter
    was undef. Declared separately, with a regression test over the
    whole id-resolution path.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:13:40 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.17) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: AI mode's own writes bypassed the account scope. ai_consent,
    ai_requests and ai_cache have RLS policies WITH CHECK, so an insert
    through a bare database handle is refused - correctly, since the GUC
    saying who is asking was never set. Consent and accounting now run
    inside Query::scoped, the same wrapper every index read uses, so
    there is one place that establishes the account scope rather than
    two.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:10:18 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.16) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Fix a 500 on the signed-in search page: the consent lookup runs on
    every render and bound its hash as bytea, which Mojo::Pg rejects
    with 'Cannot bind a reference' (and a bare scalar arrives as 47
    bytes, not 32). Migration 006 converts the column to 64 hex
    characters, which needs no driver type hint and can be checked
    against sha256sum by hand.
  * Consent notice expanded, per Silver Linings: PII is not stripped
    before sending; all users share one Anthropic workspace, which makes
    a request hard to attribute but is explicitly NOT anonymity because
    the contents carry names and addresses; the channel is encrypted end
    to end; and prompt caching is disclosed, accurately - the cached
    prefix is this service's fixed instructions, not the question or the
    mail.
  * Fix an escaped attribute in the AI result list: rel=noopener
    noreferrer was emitted through <%= %> and came out as
    rel=&quot;...&quot;, so the one attribute target=_blank requires was
    not applied.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:06:50 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.15) unstable; urgency=medium

  * AI mode (plan phase 3): an optional natural-language search over the
    person's own mail and files, off by default and dark without an API
    key.
  * Consent is recorded against a HASH of the notice text, so rewording
    it asks everyone again instead of carrying them onto new terms.
    Append-only grant/revoke log; turning it off is one POST with no
    confirmation.
  * Six server-side gates in cost order: kill switch, consent, rate
    limit, month budget, response cache, then a cheap Haiku topic gate
    before any Opus call.
  * The model returns ids, never markup. Every id is re-resolved under
    the account's own RLS scope before rendering, so an invented or
    borrowed id renders nothing.
  * Tool results are fenced and labelled as untrusted: anyone can email
    this person, and that text reaches the model looking like everything
    else.
  * Migration 005 adds six tables. The three carrying an account_id get
    RLS with WITH CHECK, unlike the read-only index tables, because the
    app writes its own records - a cross-account write is a policy
    violation, not a filed row.
  * Prices are built into the client as well as the config: the first
    hand-written ai: block on the fleet had no prices, which made every
    request cost 0 and the monthly budget unenforceable.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 23:01:01 -0500

homelab-search (0.2.14) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Filter panel reset: sections instead of a flat grid, inapplicable
    controls removed rather than greyed, source as two checkboxes, date
    presets, and type families that reveal the exact types they cover.
  * Fix: .field label outranked .chk, so every type checkbox rendered as
    a tiny uppercase muted label with its box stacked above it -
    indistinguishable from a disabled control.
  * Fix: --space-5 and --space-10 are not defined by the shared theme,
    so four declarations using them were invalid and dropped.
  * Fix: ticking an exact type under a ticked family was a no-op,
    because the server unions type_in tokens. A family is now a tri-
    state select-all for its types.
  * Mime: categories_of() returns every category a type belongs to, so
    text/csv appears under Text and Spreadsheets; new 'office' super-
    category; 'documents' relabelled 'Word processing' and no longer
    covers PDF; friendly() gives human names for the verbose Office
    types.

 -- Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>  Sat, 31 Oct 2026 22:03:29 -0500

